<edge source="us_trade_coercion::tariff_escalation_speed" target="iran_hormuz_conflict::kinetic_escalation" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A rapid pace of tariff escalation signals aggressive economic coercion and compresses diplomatic options, incentivizing Iranian authorities or proxies to employ kinetic signaling (naval confrontations, attacks on tankers) to impose costs and deter further escalation.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_coercion</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_trade_coercion::tariff_escalation_speed" target="russia_ukraine_war::alliance_cohesion" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rapid, unilateral increases in US tariffs force allied governments into immediate defensive responses (tariff exemptions, retaliatory measures, public criticism), producing visible policy divergence and reducing short-term alignment within NATO/EU coalitions.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_coercion</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_trade_coercion::tariff_escalation_speed" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::compromised_hardware_rat" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rapid escalation of tariffs forces firms to quickly shift procurement to alternative, lower‑cost suppliers (to avoid tariff exposure) and shortens supplier vetting, increasing the probability of importing backdoored or substandard hardware.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_trade_coercion</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
